Task 6: fix review findings not covered by any PR (2026-11-16)
Approach per finding: write a regression test, confirm it FAILS without the fix and PASSES with
it, run the whole suite, add an end-to-end check with the built binary where useful, one commit
per finding (or per tightly related pair). Commits authored by Christian Fiedler with a
Co-Authored-By trailer for Claude.

C5 + H7 (lock released immediately; lock needs write access) - FIXED 006a6eb.
- `let _lock = ..?` keeps the guard alive; Move arm now also propagates lock errors.
- FileLock: open for write + F_WRLCK; if that fails because the file/fs is read-only (EACCES,
  EPERM, EROFS) open for read + F_RDLCK (shared lock, still conflicts with cooperating writers).
- Test test_commands_work_on_read_only_files_with_locking: fails without the lock.rs change
  ("Failed to open file .. for write: Permission denied"), passes with it.
- strace end-to-end: F_WRLCK -> unlink -> F_UNLCK (lock now held across the operation).
- Windows (Wine): dedupe command tests 18/19 pass; the 1 failure (soft link) also fails without
  this change (Wine symlink limitation) -> not a regression. Related upstream issue: #113.
H1 (-n/--rf-over panics) - FIXED 1d0fdcf. RangedU64ValueParser::<usize>. Test parses -n for
  remove/link/dedupe/move and rejects 0; fails without the fix. End-to-end: `remove --dry-run -n 2`
  on 3 copies removes 1. Fixes upstream issue #302.
C1 (--in-place --no-copy deletes originals) - FIXED 830680d. Output::InPlace no longer deletes
  anything (the temp copy is removed by Input::Copied). Tests: no-copy keeps original (fails without
  the fix), copy mode leaves the tmp dir empty. End-to-end: all files survive `group --transform
  'touch $IN' --in-place --no-copy`.
C4 + H2 (transform output truncated; deadlock) - FIXED 05b70d1. hash_transformed hashes the
  output stream to EOF. Tests with `od`: different inputs sharing an output prefix get different
  hashes (failed without fix); 200 KB through `od -v` completes (without fix: hung until the 60 s
  timeout = deadlock reproduced). End-to-end with `gzip -dc`: differing .gz files no longer
  grouped, identical ones still grouped, no hang.
H6 (+ upstream issue #320) - FIXED d5e7081. New GroupConfig::report_filter()/reported_rf_over();
  strict filter applied at the end of group_files(), used for report stats, and passed to the
  dedupe commands (they took --rf-over 0->1 from transform reports). Tests for --transform
  singletons and --unique --skip-content-hash both fail without the final filter. End-to-end:
  #320 scenario now lists only the unique file; `--transform cat --rf-over 2` report -> remove
  keeps 2 of 3. Also fixes #320, very likely #345 (reporter gave no command line).
C2 (-S -H remove deletes symlink target) - FIXED 1325d3f. partition() keeps a regular file whose
  only kept same-inode paths are symlinks; dedupe_script() prefers a regular file as link target.
  Test covers remove, link (hard) and link --soft with the exact setup; fails without the rule.
  End-to-end: warning "Keeping b: a symbolic link to it is kept", data intact.
C3 (report reader trims paths; base dir not unescaped) - FIXED 0a11031. Only the 4-space indent
  and the line terminator are stripped; `# Base dir:` decoded with from_escaped_string (fallback
  to raw). Tests: names with leading/trailing space, U+2003, tab; base dir with backslash and
  trailing space - both fail with the old reader. End-to-end with the review scenario: the
  unrelated file "a" survives, "a " (the real duplicate) is removed. Fixes upstream issue #275.
H3 (--max-suffix-size underflow) - FIXED 56430b6. Clamp suffix length to file length. No unit
  test: whether the suffix stage runs depends on the detected device type (threshold 64 KiB SSD /
  64 MiB other) which group_files() cannot inject. End-to-end instead (ZFS here = "unknown" kind):
  two 70 MB files, --max-suffix-size 128MiB: pre-fix debug build panics "attempt to subtract with
  overflow" (release: EINVAL, seen in the review), fixed build finds the duplicate. Upstream #303.
H4 (priority top/bottom) - FIXED 1e65bd7. top/bottom are stable sorts by the position in the
  report. Test with 5 priority combinations; fails with the old reverse()/no-op.
H5 (arg::split char vs byte offsets) - FIXED 7d2f84f. char_indices(). split(join(args)) round-trip
  test with umlauts/€/emoji next to $'...' words fails with the old code. End-to-end: the review's
  --isolate U/Ü scenario now behaves identically.
H8 (regex anchoring, escaped \$) - FIXED 5f608d9. Wrap a pattern with a top-level `|` in (?:...)
  (only then, to keep the fixed-prefix directory pruning for other patterns); strip only an
  unescaped trailing `$`. Test fails with old code. End-to-end: `-x --name '.*\.png|.*\.txt'` no
  longer matches x.png.bak / y.png.old.
H9 (--exclude prefix-matches directories) - FIXED b8dcc0a. Directory excluded only on a full
  match of its path with or without trailing separator. Test (incl. /dev/** still excluding /dev)
  fails with the old rule. End-to-end: `--exclude '**/foo'` keeps foobar/.
M1 (--depth off by one) - FIXED fbab8bc. `level >= depth`. Test: depth 1 -> only top-level files,
  depth 2 -> one level of subdirs; fails with the old check.
M2 (hidden input paths skipped) - FIXED 46bbdcc. Hidden filter only for level > 0 (entries found
  while walking). Test with hidden dir and hidden file as input; fails with the old check.
M3 (.fdignore ignored with .gitignore; nested negation) - FIXED b653959. Both files loaded into one
  matcher per dir (.fdignore last); stack consulted deepest-first, first explicit match decides.
  Test (a in .gitignore, b in .fdignore, *.log ignored at root, !keep.log in sub) fails with old code.
M4 (!(...) extglob) - FIXED 04f84b9 (as "clear error"): cannot be expressed without look-around
  in the regex crate, so it is now rejected with "negated patterns !(...) are not supported; use
  --exclude instead" and the rustdoc no longer advertises it (README never listed it). Test added.
M5 + M6 (transform pre-flight; command parsing) - FIXED 2ab4e36. Program resolved like the OS does
  (path as given / PATH + PATHEXT) without executing it; no panic on non-UTF-8 names.
  parse_command keeps a literal `$`, keeps unknown `$NAME`, trims, and errors on unparsed input.
  Tests: literal-dollar parsing; script outside PATH accepted, missing program rejected. (The old
  code cannot compile against the new tests because parse_command's signature changed, so
  before/after was shown end-to-end: absolute script path now accepted; `sed -e s/one$/two/` now
  works and makes a/b duplicates.) Related upstream issue #288.
M7 (hard links inflate redundant/reclaimed sizes) - FIXED a596772. FileGroup::redundant_size()
  counts distinct inodes not reachable from kept files; redundant_count fast path not used with
  group_by_id; run_script/log_script count freed space per inode only when all its links were
  processed. Tests for both. End-to-end (3 links + copy, 1000 B): before "Redundant: 3000 B" /
  "reclaim 3.0 KB", now 1000 B / 1.0 KB. Related upstream issues #268, #257.
M8 (dry-run scripts) - FIXED 2f0090b. Move scripts start with `mkdir -p <target dir>` (Windows:
  `if not exist .. mkdir ..`); Windows `mklink [/H] <link> <target>` order fixed. Unix test runs
  the printed script with `sh -e` (fails without mkdir); Windows test verified under Wine; both fail
  with the old code. Fixes upstream issue #367.
M9 (fixed-prefix extraction / partial match) - FIXED 03c3742. Escape flag reset after one char,
  char-based pop before ?/*/{, char-based truncation in is_partial_match. 3 new tests fail with the
  old code. Adjusted the existing test_unicode: it asserted `!is_partial_match("ąęść---")` for
  `^ąęść?`, which only held because of the byte/char bug - the same bug produced false negatives
  (e.g. "ąęś/" rejected although "ąęś" matches); partial matching may give false positives only.
M10 (get_mount_point panic) - FIXED 003547f. Returns Option; None when unknown (move then tries
  rename with copy fallback as before). Test with empty and nested mount lists.
M11 + upstream issue #84 (mtime-only staleness checks) - FIXED 46f62e8. Dedupe skips groups whose
  files have ctime (Unix) newer than the report; the hash cache records and compares ctime (new tree
  `hash_db_v2`, old entries ignored instead of failing to deserialize). Tests for both fail without
  the change. End-to-end with the exact #84 reproduction (cp -a Z 1 after group, then link): before
  -> all three files "abcd" (data loss), now the group is skipped with a warning, files intact.
  Trade-off recorded in the commit: content-preserving operations that change ctime (fclones
  link/dedupe themselves, chmod, chown) invalidate cache entries and make later dedupe runs skip
  those groups with a warning. Chosen because a stale cached hash is a data-loss path that the
  dedupe-time check cannot catch (the file changed before the group run).
M12 (reflink of files with different sizes) - FIXED aac8ef6. reflink() compares the current
  sizes and refuses if they differ (reachable with --transform reports where size checks are off;
  FICLONE could leave the old tail of a larger destination). Linux/transform semantics otherwise
  unchanged (README does not promise more; with #299 the kernel verifies contents where
  FIDEDUPERANGE is supported). Updated 2 existing tests that used different-size files: one now
  uses same-size different-content files with a deterministic expectation (kernel refusal when
  FIDEDUPERANGE is available on the ioctl path, full clone otherwise), the other expects refusal on
  every path (it previously expected "too large" to be replaced by "foo" on the non-ioctl path).
M13 (malformed group header panics) - FIXED 3a6acc7. Errors instead of unwrap(); test with odd-length
  hash and overflowing numbers fails (panics) with the old parser.
L1 + L11 + L15 - FIXED 793b9a2 (is_empty inverted assert; StdLog without current_exe; typos,
  numbered "Fetching extents" phase label, Pattern docs). Test for is_empty.
L4 - FIXED 7d2668a (quote '~' and '!'; fixes upstream #366). Test fails with old list.

(Work was paused here once because of a usage limit and resumed afterwards.)
L5 - FIXED 5db34e4. sort_by_cached_key (key computed once per item, each error reported once);
  doc corrected (failed keys sort first). Test fails with the old sort_by_key.
L6 - FIXED 3bc7bae. strip_prefix returns None if the base is longer. Test case added.
L7 - FIXED 0017e6b (Unix part; Windows part = PR #365). from_escaped_string rejects paths
  decoding to NUL (returns String error now; all callers only format it). End-to-end with a
  crafted report: old build panics, new build reports "path contains a NUL character".
L9 - FIXED be849aa. --isolate with --stdin used to be rejected (validation counted positional
  paths only). It also could not have worked: roots and walk both read stdin, the first reader got
  everything -> nothing scanned (seen end-to-end). Now validate() skips the count for --stdin,
  run_group() reads the stdin list once when --isolate is set, and the dedupe commands refuse to
  rebuild roots from such a report (they would read the report itself as path list) and ask for
  explicit --isolate roots. End-to-end: group finds the cross-root group; remove without roots ->
  clear error; with roots -> removes the whole 2/ subgroup.
L10 - FIXED 3378e9c. read_dir entry errors and file-type errors are logged as warnings. No test
  (cannot be provoked portably).
L12 - FIXED ed6f223. RLIM_INFINITY no longer wraps to -1 (clamped). Helper + test. Not changed: a
  transform child uses ~3 fds per semaphore permit (left as is, documented in the review).
L13 - FIXED 2e35e75. min(--max-prefix-size, device minimum prefix). Test with --skip-content-hash
  and 1000-byte prefix. Note: my first "fails without fix" check did not apply because cargo fmt
  had reformatted the line; re-done against the real old code: the test fails there.
L14 - FIXED c614bb3. Transform no longer derives Clone (Drop deletes its temp dir).
L2 + L3 - FIXED bd76f19. --threads removable:N used for removable disks; device-name regex
  handles multi-letter sd*, vd*, xvd*, mmcblk*. Tests; device-name test fails with the old regex.
L8 - NOT FIXED (decision): groups decided without reading contents (hard-link-only groups with -H,
  single files in --unique reports) show hash 0. Consistent existing design; computing a hash only
  for display would read data for nothing; fclones does not use the field for correctness.
